  "evidence_url": "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/c730051590dce08c1ede987497d6e45efb2f839a",
  "explanation": "byteorder-lite 0.1.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. The four variable-width unsigned writers additionally have exact byte-count/space guards, selected-byte output on success and assertion failure otherwise.",
  "limitations": "This covers 24 selected writer methods and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Reads, remaining signed variable-width wrappers, bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified.",
